# Testing at seams

## Seam

A seam is the public boundary under test (port, service API, HTTP handler, CLI).
Tests cross that boundary only. Confirm seams with the spec (or user) before adding tests.

## Do

- Assert observable behavior and return values / visible side effects through the seam
- Expected values from an independent source (literal, example, Done when) — not re-implemented production logic
- Vertical slices: one failing test → minimal code → repeat
- Prefer fakes/adapters at seams over mocking private collaborators

## Do not

- Test private methods or internal key layouts unless the spec says that layout is the contract
- Tautological asserts (`expect(f(x)).toBe(sameFormulaAsProduction(x))`)
- Write the entire suite first (horizontal slicing), then all implementation
- Treat refactoring as part of red→green — keep green, refactor after or in review

## If no seam in spec

For pure infra/config tickets: skip heavy tests or smoke at the only real boundary (e.g. healthcheck). Do not invent seams for decoration.
